// Self-host Stage 2 (#1339): the size-budgeted inlining pass — the DECISION
// half. Split out of opt.bit (#1503), then out of optinline.bit (#4574) when
// the emitted-cost model below took the file past 800 lines. optinline.bit
// keeps the transform; everything here answers "may this callee be spliced,
// and what does it cost".
//
// EVERY JUDGEMENT HERE IS ABOUT THE CALLEE AS IT WILL BE EMITTED, not as it
// sits in the snapshot (#4574). A callee whose own calls this same splice will
// flatten emits none of them, so it is priced as the leaf it becomes, at the
// size it becomes — see `calleeEmittedCount` and `calleeEmitsNoCall`. Judging
// the snapshot body instead left `bit_rt_port_sched_worker_at` (9 snapshot
// instructions, one `call taskAt`) out of line at every one of its 12 static
// call sites: it read as a non-leaf, drew maxInlineInsts() = 8, and 9 > 8,
// while the body a splice would actually have emitted is 11 instructions with
// no call at all — inside maxInlineLeafInsts() = 16.

// The splice budget for a callee that itself CALLS something, in IR
// instructions of its own body (calleeInstCount).
//
// 8 is measured, not a default. #4344 swept it uniformly at 8/16/32/48/64 over
// the tree-compiled aarch64-macos runtime archive and found the static `bl`
// count RISES past 8 — br26 in runtime_root.o 789 -> 892 at 16, the archive
// +8.6% — because a bigger callee brings its own calls to every site it is
// spliced into, which lengthens the splice CHAIN and pushes the leaves past
// maxInlineDepth(). Raising THIS number buys calls, it does not remove them.
fn maxInlineInsts(): int {
  return 8
}

// The splice budget for a callee that calls NOTHING (calleeIsLeaf).
// Deliberately larger, and the reason is the one #4344's sweep measured but did
// not separate: every cost that made a uniform raise lose is a cost of the
// callee's OWN calls.
//
// A leaf carries no call to the site it lands in, so it cannot lengthen a
// chain, cannot push another callee past maxInlineDepth(), and cannot hand an
// out-of-object call to a new object. Its whole cost is its own body, once per
// site, and that is exactly what calleeInstCount already measures. One budget
// was pricing a leaf at the rate a non-leaf's chain growth deserves.
//
// #4420 re-ran #4344's table with this split, same method (a stage0-built
// compiler per row, then `BIT=<that> scripts/g2archive.sh aarch64-macos`), on
// a820b711. `br26` counts ARM64_RELOC_BRANCH26 relocations — the static call
// sites — `_root` in runtime_root.o and `_all` over all 25 objects:
//
//   leaf   archive  br26_root  ->_scalarHash  br26_all  insts   json_bytes  json_bl
//     8    906922       546          8          2092    47473     351731      1371
//    13    909034       538          0          2032    47701     351540      1332
//    16    909034       538          0          2032    47701     351540      1332
//    24    913898       535          0          2009    48133     351540      1317
//
// Row 8 reproduces main byte for byte, which is what makes the rest a
// measurement rather than four builds. Calls now FALL instead of rising: -60
// static call sites for +0.23% of bytes, against #4344's uniform 16 which
// bought +103 calls for +8.6%. 13 and 16 are the SAME build — nothing in this
// corpus is a leaf of 14..16 IR instructions — so 16 costs nothing over the 13
// `runtime/root/maps.bit`'s `scalarHash` needs (splitmix64's finaliser: one
// block, no branches, no memory, 13 IR instructions of which 5 are `const_int`,
// 8 `bl` sites in runtime_root.o) and is not a constant fitted to one function.
// 24 keeps improving (-83 calls, +0.77% bytes); its own ticket, its own cycles.
//
// #4574: "a leaf" is `calleeEmitsNoCall`, which reads the body a splice would
// EMIT. The argument above is about what the caller receives, and a callee
// whose only call this same splice flattens hands the caller no call either —
// so it earns this budget on the argument's own terms. `calleeEmittedCount`
// then charges it for the flattened body it really emits, not for the smaller
// snapshot one, so the two changes are a re-reading of the same rule rather
// than a loosening of it.
fn maxInlineLeafInsts(): int {
  return 16
}

// How many levels of callee body one call site may absorb (#3163). Level 1 is
// the callee itself; level 2 is a call INSIDE that spliced body, and so on.
//
// This bound is what makes the pass terminate, and it is the only thing that
// does: `optimizeModule` resolves every callee out of a PRE-INLINING snapshot,
// so a recursive or mutually recursive cycle would otherwise splice forever —
// a call graph with a cycle has no callee-first order and no fixpoint to reach.
// A self-call is refused outright by inlineEligible's `calleeIdx == callerIdx`
// test, which also covers every cycle that runs back through the original
// caller; this bound covers the rest.
//
// 2, measured rather than guessed (#3163), on the tree-built aarch64-macos
// runtime archive and a whole-module `stdlib/crypto` object:
//
//        runtime_gc.o   bl / to wordAt    libbitrt.a    stdlib/crypto.o
//   main   54472 bytes    222 /  29        487072 b      1237610 b / 10815 bl
//   d=1    55265          239 /  61        496616        1235026   / 10772
//   d=2    54761          171 /   0        496288        1238410   / 10827
//   d=3    54761          171 /   0        497136        1238410   / 10827
//
// 1 is not enough and is WORSE than no fix at all on the case this exists for:
// resolving callees from the snapshot stops the collector's `loadWord` from
// arriving pre-flattened, so inlining it merely COPIES its `call wordAt` to
// more sites — 29 -> 61. Only the second level collapses them, to 0.
//
// 3 buys nothing: `runtime_gc.o` and `stdlib/crypto.o` come out byte-identical
// to d=2, and the archive grows 848 bytes. There is nothing left to collapse
// because an eligible body is under maxInlineInsts() and the leaves are reached
// at the second level.
//
// Growth is bounded by the per-callee budget at each level, so the worst case a
// call site can absorb is maxInlineInsts()^(maxInlineDepth()-1) *
// maxInlineLeafInsts(): only the LAST level can be a leaf, because a leaf
// contains no call for the next level to reach.
fn maxInlineDepth(): int {
  return 2
}

// The index of the function named `name`, or -1. The seed keys calls by FuncId;
// selfhost resolves the callee name into `IrInstr.name`, so the lookup is here.
//
// Takes the function list rather than the module because the inliner must read
// callees out of a snapshot taken before this pass mutated anything (#3163),
// never out of the module it is halfway through rewriting.
fn funcIndexByName(funcs: []IrFunc, name: string): int {
  let i = 0
  while (i < len(funcs)) {
    if (funcs[i].name == name) {
      return i
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return -1
}

// A callee is inlinable when it is an ACYCLIC body under budget: every block
// leaves by `jump`, `br` or `ret` and every `jump`/`br` goes forward
// (calleeShapeOk), with at least one `ret` so there is a value to substitute,
// not fallible (keeps error propagation out of this pass), and not the caller
// itself (never inline a self-call). It must also not carry another module's
// MODULE STATE into this one — see importsForeignState (#1630) — and neither
// side may be @naked (#1636).
//
// §10.3.1 @naked is a codegen contract, not a hint: codegen emits NO prologue
// and NO epilogue, so the body is raw machine code that owns the frame itself
// and ends the call with its own `ret` (usually inside `asm volatile`).
// Splicing such a body into a caller is never correct in either direction:
//
//   - a naked CALLEE spliced into a normal caller drops that raw `ret` in the
//     middle of the caller, which then returns with its callee-saved registers
//     and link register never restored — a hang or a SIGSEGV with a perfectly
//     clean build, and every instruction after the call site left unreachable.
//     That was live: bit_rt_root_safepoint disappeared from every call site
//     (#1636), and it becomes universal at G2 (#1583), when the backends'
//     synthesized back-edge call starts reaching a naked shim.
//   - a normal callee spliced INTO a naked caller adds instructions to a
//     function that has no frame to spend: regalloc runs a naked function on a
//     reduced register set with zero spill slots, so extra pressure trips the
//     "no frame" assertion in both backends at best.
//
// The refusal is on the ATTRIBUTE, not on a cost heuristic — a naked body small
// enough to fit the budget is exactly the dangerous case.
//
// @nosplit is deliberately NOT refused. It only suppresses the BACK-EDGE
// safepoint (codegen.bit's needsNoSafepoints), and an inlinable callee is
// ACYCLIC — calleeShapeOk refuses a back edge outright, see calleeExitOk for
// why that refusal is load-bearing rather than conservative — so splicing it
// neither introduces a poll into nosplit code nor removes one from a caller
// that keeps its own back edges. Refusing it would also disable inlining
// across the whole runtime, where §17.6 makes every function @nosplit.
//
// Both functions are read from `snap`, the pre-inlining snapshot (#3163), and
// so is every callee `calleeEmittedCount` prices underneath them. That is what
// keeps eligibility independent of how much a callee had already absorbed by
// the time this caller was reached — the filename-ordering bug. #4574 does NOT
// weaken it: the emitted shape is DERIVED from the snapshot by asking
// `splicedNestedIdx` what this splice would do, never read out of the module
// the pass is halfway through rewriting.

// True if block `bi` of `g` leaves the block in a way a splice can reproduce:
// a `ret` (which becomes a jump into the join block), or a `jump`/`br` to a
// LATER block.
//
// "Later" is the acyclicity test, and it is a block-id comparison because a
// snapshot function's block ids ascend with its linear schedule — `rebuild`
// (optrebuild.bit) reserves blocks in `blockEmissionOrder` and every callee
// here comes out of phase 1's fold+DCE, so `target <= bi` is exactly a back
// edge. Same rule as codegen.bit's `isBackEdge`.
//
// #4344: REFUSING A LOOP IS WHAT KEEPS THE @nosplit REASONING BELOW TRUE.
// §10.3.1 @nosplit suppresses only the BACK-EDGE safepoint poll, so it is a
// property of the loops a function contains, not of its call sites. Splice a
// LOOPING callee and that stops composing in both directions: a @nosplit
// caller swallowing a polling callee's loop silently deletes the poll (the
// collector then waits on a thread that never reaches a safepoint), and a
// polling caller swallowing a @nosplit callee's loop silently adds one to code
// that declared it must not have one. An acyclic callee introduces no back
// edge at all, so it neither adds nor removes a poll and the attribute keeps
// meaning what it says — a refusal on the DATA SHAPE rather than a pairwise
// attribute rule at the call site.
//
// `unreachable` is refused with everything else by falling off the end: there
// is no join edge to build from a block that never leaves.
fn calleeExitOk(g: IrFunc, bi: int): bool {
  let blk = g.blocks[bi]
  // An empty block has no terminator, and `instsStart + instsLen - 1` would
  // name the instruction BEFORE it — index -1 for block 0 (opt.bit's
  // `computeReachable` guards the same read for the same reason).
  if (blk.instsLen == 0) {
    return false
  }
  let t = g.instrs[blk.instsStart + blk.instsLen - 1]
  if (t.op == Op.Ret) {
    return true
  }
  if (t.op == Op.Jump) {
    return t.ival > bi
  }
  if (t.op == Op.Br) {
    return t.ival > bi && t.ival2 > bi
  }
  return false
}

// True if `g`'s own shape is inlinable, independent of the call site: an
// ACYCLIC body every one of whose blocks leaves by `ret`, `jump` or `br`
// (`calleeExitOk`), with at least one `ret` so there is a value to substitute
// and an edge for the join block to receive, and not fallible (keeps error
// propagation out of this pass). Split out of inlineEligible to keep it under
// E0204's complexity ceiling once the #3755 arity check was added.
//
// #4344 lifted the old `len(g.blocks) != 1` refusal. One block meant the
// splice needed no CFG surgery at all, and the cost was that `&&`, `||` and a
// single early `return` — which lower to two blocks each — put a function
// permanently out of reach: `runtime/root/root.bit`'s `strSize` (`if (s == 0)
// { return 0 }; return loadWord(...)`) was 33 `bl`s and `strBytes` 41 in
// `runtime_root.o` alone, and `stdlib/json/lex.bit`'s `isDigit`
// (`c >= '0' && c <= '9'`) was a call per character scanned.
fn calleeShapeOk(g: IrFunc): bool {
  if (g.isFallible || len(g.blocks) == 0) {
    return false
  }
  let rets = 0
  let i = 0
  while (i < len(g.blocks)) {
    if (!calleeExitOk(g, i)) {
      return false
    }
    let blk = g.blocks[i]
    if (g.instrs[blk.instsStart + blk.instsLen - 1].op == Op.Ret) {
      rets = rets + 1
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return rets > 0
}

// The size a splice of `g` costs, in instructions actually emitted: every
// block's body, excluding its params (they bind to already-emitted values or
// become block params of a block that is minted either way) and its terminator
// (a `ret` becomes a jump, a `jump`/`br` is copied — either way one branch the
// call it replaces was paying for anyway, plus the `bl`, the prologue and the
// epilogue this deletes).
fn calleeInstCount(g: IrFunc): int {
  let n = 0
  let i = 0
  while (i < len(g.blocks)) {
    n = n + g.blocks[i].instsLen - g.blocks[i].paramCount - 1
    i = i + 1
  }
  return n
}

// The snapshot index of the callee at instruction `idx` of `g` when a splice
// walking `g`'s body at `depth` will replace that instruction with the
// callee's body, and -1 otherwise.
//
// THE ONLY DEFINITION OF "THIS CALL WILL BE FLATTENED" (#4574). `spliceNested`
// asks it what to do; `calleeEmittedCount` and `calleeEmitsNoCall` ask it what
// a splice will look like. One function, so a budget can never be spent
// against a shape the splicer would not have produced — the two used to be
// separate readings of the same rule, and the cost model was the stale one.
//
// `depth` is the depth of the BODY being walked, matching `spliceBlockBody`'s
// parameter: a call written in the original caller's own source is walked at
// 0, a call carried in on a callee's body at 1, and `maxInlineDepth()` is the
// first depth at which nothing is spliced at all.
fn splicedNestedIdx(ic: InlineCtx, g: IrFunc, idx: int, depth: int): int {
  if (depth >= maxInlineDepth()) {
    return -1
  }
  let ins = g.instrs[idx]
  if (ins.op != Op.Call) {
    return -1
  }
  let ci = funcIndexByName(ic.snap, ins.name)
  if (!inlineEligible(ic, ci, len(ins.args), depth)) {
    return -1
  }
  if (!retWidthAgrees(g, idx, ic.snap[ci])) {
    return -1
  }
  return ci
}

// What a splice of `g` at `depth` actually emits, in instructions: its own
// body (`calleeInstCount`) plus, for every call the SAME splice will flatten
// into it, that callee's body in place of the instruction slots the call
// occupied — the `Op.Call` and its `Op.CallWord` run (`callWordRunLen`), which
// `bindSplicedResult` binds and emits nothing for.
//
// Judging `calleeInstCount(g)` alone under-prices exactly the callees a nested
// splice grows and over-prices the ones it flattens; this is the same number
// for a callee with no flattenable call, so no leaf's price moves.
//
// TERMINATION. The recursion through `splicedNestedIdx` -> `inlineEligible` ->
// here is bounded by `maxInlineDepth()` and by nothing else: each level asks
// about `depth + 1`, and at `maxInlineDepth()` `splicedNestedIdx` returns -1
// before it looks at the instruction. That is the same bound, read the same
// way, that makes the splice itself terminate on a recursive call graph
// (#3163) — the model cannot run deeper than the transform it models.
fn calleeEmittedCount(ic: InlineCtx, g: IrFunc, depth: int): int {
  let n = calleeInstCount(g)
  let i = 0
  while (i < len(g.instrs)) {
    let ci = splicedNestedIdx(ic, g, i, depth + 1)
    if (ci >= 0) {
      n = n + calleeInstCount(ic.snap[ci]) - callWordRunLen(g, i)
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return n
}

// True if a splice of `g` at `depth` emits no call at all, so it hands the
// caller nothing for the next level of `spliceNested` to reach.
//
// This is the leaf test, taken on the body that will be EMITTED rather than on
// the snapshot (#4574): an `Op.Call` this splice will flatten leaves nothing
// behind, so it does not disqualify `g`. `runtime/sched/worker.bit`'s
// `schedWorkerAt` is the case — one `call taskAt`, and `taskAt` is three
// instructions of pointer arithmetic that the same splice inlines.
//
// Every op that transfers control to code this function does not contain, and
// no others: `Op.FuncAddr` and `Op.MakeClosure` only NAME a function, and
// `Op.CallWord` is a word of the call it follows, already counted by it.
// `Op.Asm` is in the set because an `asm volatile` body is opaque — a budget
// that assumed otherwise would be a claim about text this pass never reads.
// None of those five is ever flattened, so each is a refusal outright; only
// `Op.Call` gets to ask whether it survives.
fn calleeEmitsNoCall(ic: InlineCtx, g: IrFunc, depth: int): bool {
  let i = 0
  while (i < len(g.instrs)) {
    let op = g.instrs[i].op
    if (op == Op.CallValue ||
      op == Op.CallIface ||
      op == Op.RtCall ||
      op == Op.Syscall ||
      op == Op.Asm) {
      return false
    }
    if (op == Op.Call && splicedNestedIdx(ic, g, i, depth + 1) < 0) {
      return false
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return true
}

// The budget `g` is judged against at `depth`: see maxInlineLeafInsts for why
// a splice that emits no call gets its own, larger one.
fn calleeEmittedBudget(ic: InlineCtx, g: IrFunc, depth: int): int {
  if (calleeEmitsNoCall(ic, g, depth)) {
    return maxInlineLeafInsts()
  }
  return maxInlineInsts()
}

fn inlineEligible(
  ic: InlineCtx, calleeIdx: int, argCount: int, depth: int,
): bool {
  if (calleeIdx < 0 || calleeIdx == ic.callerIdx) {
    return false
  }
  let g = ic.snap[calleeIdx]
  let f = ic.snap[ic.callerIdx]
  if (g.isNaked || f.isNaked) {
    return false
  }
  if (!calleeShapeOk(g)) {
    return false
  }
  // §14: a call whose argument count differs from the callee's entry-block
  // paramCount only reaches the optimizer via --dump-ir on checker-rejected
  // input (E0050) — bit check/build/run refuse it before this pass ever runs.
  // Declining the splice here, rather than clamping spliceCallee's binding
  // loop, is what keeps every param slot bound to a real argument; a clamp
  // would leave the unbound tail holding value id 0, which is silently wrong
  // IR rather than a refusal (#3755).
  if (g.blocks[0].paramCount != argCount) {
    return false
  }
  if (importsForeignState(ic.m, f, g)) {
    return false
  }
  if (importsForeignCall(ic.snap, f, g)) {
    return false
  }
  return calleeEmittedCount(ic, g, depth) <= calleeEmittedBudget(ic, g, depth)
}

// True if splicing `callee` into `caller` would move a reference to a module
// cell across a module boundary (#1630). Mirrors the seed's importsForeignState.
//
// §11.11 state is a per-module cell, and §17.6 gives each module its OWN object:
// the cell is defined by that object and by no other. Inlining is the one thing
// that can put a GlobalAddr naming module M's cell inside code emitted as module
// N's — and then N's object either re-defines M's cell (two cells, one logical
// global, each module updating its own — silent, unbounded wrongness with no
// diagnostic at any stage) or leaves a dangling reference under a name M's own
// object does not use. That was live: the inliner spliced runtime/root's pinned
// rootBindMemory into runtime/root/darwin's boot, so the two objects held
// separate gcAddr and gcState cells and the collector booted against the wrong
// one.
//
// Refused unconditionally rather than only under --freestanding, so the managed
// and freestanding lowerings of one program cannot disagree about which cell a
// function touches. The cost is one un-inlined call in a cross-module helper
// that reads module state.
//
// The comparison is over inRootModule because that is the module identity the IR
// carries. It is coarse — two distinct imported modules both read as "not root"
// — but it composes: a splice into a non-root caller leaves a non-root global
// reference behind, which this then refuses to carry into any root caller.
fn importsForeignState(m: IrModule, caller: IrFunc, callee: IrFunc): bool {
  let i = 0
  while (i < len(callee.instrs)) {
    let ins = callee.instrs[i]
    if (ins.op == Op.GlobalAddr) {
      let gi = 0
      while (gi < len(m.globals)) {
        if (m.globals[gi].name == ins.name && m.globals[gi].inRootModule != caller.inRootModule) {
          return true
        }
        gi = gi + 1
      }
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return false
}

// True if splicing `callee` into `caller` would move a reference to a
// module-PRIVATE FUNCTION across a module boundary (#3163). The call-graph
// counterpart of importsForeignState, and refused for the same reason.
//
// §17.6 + §11.9: a freestanding object emits only the ROOT module's functions,
// so an outbound reference is resolvable only under a name a sibling object can
// define — a §11.9 pin, a bit_rt_* runtime symbol or a §11.7 extern, all of
// which are '$'-free. An unpinned import is spelled `m<id>$f` with `<id>` an
// ordinal THIS build assigned, and the sibling object that defines that function
// emits it BARE, because it is the root there; the two spellings can never meet.
// emit.bit's firstUnpinnedImport refuses exactly that, and this mirrors both its
// op set (Call and FuncAddr are the references an object emits) and its
// name-based pinned test.
//
// The inliner is the one pass that can manufacture such a reference from
// correct input, and it is not hypothetical: `bit_rt_port_mem_load_byte` is
// pinned, so calling it across a module is legal, but its BODY calls `byteAt`
// (runtime/gc/mem.bit:143), which is private to runtime/gc. Splicing that body
// into a caller in runtime/root/linux made that object reference `m11$byteAt`,
// which nothing defines — `g2archive.sh` refused the emit for two of its 23
// modules. Whether the old inliner tripped this was pure luck of file order,
// which is the same accident #3163 is about: the callee had to have been
// flattened past its own budget FIRST for the splice to be declined.
//
// The comparison is over inRootModule, matching importsForeignState, and is
// coarse in the same way — two distinct imported modules both read "not root" —
// and composes for the same reason: a splice into a non-root caller leaves a
// non-root private reference behind, which this then refuses to carry into any
// root caller.
//
// Refusing is always safe: the cost is one un-inlined call. Note it declines a
// case a deeper splice would have made legal (the private call could itself be
// inlined away), because that is only knowable after the recursion has run.
//
// #3181: Op.MakeClosure carries a callee name too (see emit.bit's
// firstUnpinnedImport for where lowerStructMethodValue puts an unpinned
// cross-module symbol there — an uncalled method VALUE `v.m`) and is
// deliberately NOT added to this check's op set. Splicing a MakeClosure across
// the root/non-root boundary is provably harmless: a managed (non-freestanding)
// build links the whole program as one unit, so the mangled name resolves
// regardless of which module "owns" it, and a --freestanding build can never
// emit the spliced-in MakeClosure at all — emit.bit's refuseManagedMetadata
// unconditionally refuses ANY MakeClosure in a root-emitted function, spliced
// or hand-written, pinned or not, before refuseUnpinnedImport ever inspects it.
// Verified with a mutation: a small pinned cross-module callee whose body takes
// a same-module method value inlines here today (unblocked), and the
// subsequent --freestanding build still correctly fails with "allocates on the
// managed heap"; padding that same callee past maxInlineInsts() removes its
// eligibility, the splice does not happen, and the identical build succeeds —
// so the failure is attributable to the splice, and the splice is always
// safety-netted downstream regardless of this function.
fn importsForeignCall(snap: []IrFunc, caller: IrFunc, callee: IrFunc): bool {
  let i = 0
  while (i < len(callee.instrs)) {
    let ins = callee.instrs[i]
    if (ins.op == Op.Call || ins.op == Op.FuncAddr) {
      if (!isPinnedName(ins.name)) {
        let idx = funcIndexByName(snap, ins.name)
        if (idx < 0 || snap[idx].inRootModule != caller.inRootModule) {
          return true
        }
      }
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return false
}

